<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">South African Defence Force</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1957">1957</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The South African Defence Force (SADF) was the armed forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> from 1957 to 1994. It fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-border-war">South African Border War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a>, the Angolan government and Cuban forces, and conducted raids into neighbouring states. In 1994 it was merged with the ANC and PAC <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> armies and homeland forces to form the South African National Defence Force.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-african-defence-force.jpg" alt="South African Defence Force" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jetijones; Htonl; derivative work by Fry1989 06:14, 24 November 2011 (UTC); derivative work by Fry1989 03:29, 25 November 2011 (UTC) … · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ensign_of_the_South_African_Defence_Force_(1981%E2%80%931994).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before 1957 the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> relied on small standing cadres and expanded with white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a> in wartime: in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-defence-force-south-africa">Union Defence Force</a> began with only 3,353 full-time soldiers, yet Jan Christiaan Smuts raised 345,049 men for service abroad, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> among other places. The Defence Act (No. 44) of 1957 replaced it with the South African Defence Force, shortly before the country became a republic in 1961. Facing the rise of African nationalism, the force grew to about 25,000 men by 1963.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The force had a dual mission: to counter insurgency in every form and to keep a conventional arm able to defend the borders and strike back. Its general staff had six sections, and the South African Medical Service stood as an equal beside the army, navy and air force. Only white South Africans were conscripted, for one year at first and for two years from 1977, and the army's permanent force was 85% <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> speaking. From 1971 black battalions were raised on a tribal basis with white officers; black soldiers were first commissioned only in 1986. The End Conscription Campaign opposed the call-up.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> imposed arms <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> in 1977, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> built a domestic arms industry that made jet fighters, drones, guided missiles and armoured cars. Its units fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angolan-civil-war">Angolan Civil War</a> in Operation Savannah and alongside Rhodesian forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodesian-bush-war">Rhodesian Bush War</a>, both campaigns strategically unsuccessful. Under Pieter Willem Botha, defence minister and state president from 1984, the force numbered some 83,400 men, with auxiliaries such as 32 Battalion, the South West African Territorial Force and four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">Bantustan</a> armies.</p><p>Botha's government justified a more aggressive stance against a claimed &quot;total onslaught&quot; from abroad. The force raided suspected bases of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanist-congress-of-azania">Pan Africanist Congress</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, backed RENAMO in Mozambique and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, and by late 1985 kept 30,000 personnel on the Namibian border, often crossing into southern Angola. Before the amalgamation of 1994 it counted about 585,000 people, most of them part-time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> had also built nuclear weapons, and the stockpile was dismantled during the transition of the early 1990s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “South African Defence Force” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>South African Defence Force</b> grew into the strongest military in sub-Saharan Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Military</a></li></ul></section>
